["```python
from collections import defaultdict

# Define the PersonRecord structure
class PersonRecord:
    def __init__(self, name: str, age: int, address: str, contacts: defaultdict[str, defaultdict[str, defaultdict[str, str]]]):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age
        self.address = address
        self.contacts = contacts

# Define the address structure
class Address:
    def __init__(self, city: str, postcode: str, street: str):
        self.city = city
        self.postcode = postcode
        self.street = street

# Define the contact structure
class Contact:
    def __init__(self, email: str, phone: str):
        self.email = email
        self.phone = phone

# Define the person record structure
class PersonRecord:
    def __init__(self, name: str, age: int, address: str, contacts: defaultdict[str, defaultdict[str, defaultdict[str, str]]]):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age
        self.address = address
        self.contacts = contacts

# Define the person record for Linus Liskov
linus_record = PersonRecord(
    name="Linus Liskov",
    age=32,
    address="5 Rue Lafayette, Kraków",
    contacts={
        "contacts": {
            "email": {"email": "linus@liskov.com", "title": "Email"},
            "phone": {"email": "linus@linus.com", "title": "Phone"}
        }
    }
)

# Extract the person record
person_record = PersonRecord(
    name="Linus Liskov",
    age=32,
    address="5 Rue Lafayette, Kraków",
    contacts={
        "contacts": {
            "email": {"email": "linus@liskov.com", "title": "Email"},
            "phone": {"email": "linus@linus.com", "title": "Phone"}
        }
    }
)

# Print the person record
print(person_record)
```

This code defines a PersonRecord structure, an Address structure, a Contact structure, and a PersonRecord structure. It then creates a PersonRecord for Linus Liskov and extracts it. The extracted PersonRecord is printed to the console."]

